course: dictionary
lesson: 14687
title: Presuppose: the hidden starting idea
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: To presuppose something is to take it for granted as true before you start, or to need it to be true first. «The plan presupposes that everyone will help.» The plan only works if everyone helps, so help is its hidden starting idea.
explain: The second meaning is about needing something to exist first. «Reading presupposes knowing the letters.» You cannot read without knowing letters, so the letters must come first.
explain: Sneaky questions use this word too. «When did you stop cheating?» presupposes that you cheated, even though nobody has said so yet. To check, ask: can I answer this without agreeing to a hidden idea? If not, the question presupposes it.
fact: Presuppose means to take something for granted as true before you start, or to need something to be true first.
fact: Presuppose is a formal word, so you will meet it more in writing and arguments than in everyday talk.
fact: In a question, the presupposition is the hidden idea the question assumes: «When did you stop cheating?» presupposes that you cheated.
fact: Two useful phrases are «presuppose that» and «presupposes knowledge of».
fact: Presuppose is a verb: presuppose, presupposes, presupposed, presupposing.
exercise: «Baking a cake presupposes that you have an oven.» What does presupposes mean here? | Makes the oven bigger | Is bad for the oven | *Needs something to be true first | Forgets about the oven
exercise: «The question presupposes that Sam was late.» What does the question assume before it starts? | Sam never comes to school | *Sam was late | Nobody knows the time | Sam was early
exercise: Mia says, «Our team will win, so we presuppose that we practised hard.» What does presuppose mean in this sentence? | *Take it as already true, without checking | Forget about it | Shout it loudly to the team | Argue that it is wrong
test: «Reading presupposes knowing the letters.» Which must come first? | The book | Reading | Writing a story | *Knowing the letters
test: «When did you stop lying?» What does this question presuppose? | *You lied before | Lying is fun | You never lied | Nobody noticed anything
exam: «The science test presupposes that you can read the questions.» What must be true first? | Your teacher is kind | The test is easy | *You can read the questions | It is Friday
